# What is "~id" in Gremlin?

"~id" is a special keyword in gremlin. "~id" allows for direct lookup of a vertex or edge by it's value.
"~id" can be supplied by a user or generated by aerospike-graph-service for vertices.
"~id" is generated by aerospike-graph-service for edges (cannot be user supplied).
T.id is an alias for "~id".
"id" is not a special keyword and is just a regular property.

# Example

```
g.addV("person").property(T.id, 1).property("name", "Lyndon").iterate();  // Creates vertex with label 'person', ~id of 1, and property 'name' with value 'Lyndon'
g.addV("person").property(T.id, 2).property("name", "Grant").iterate();   // Creates vertex with label 'person', ~id of 2, and property 'name' with value 'Grant'
g.addV("person").property(T.id, 3).property("name", "Simon").iterate();   // Creates vertex with label 'person', ~id of 3, and property 'name' with value 'Simon'
g.addV("person").property(T.id, 3).property("name", "Joe").iterate();     // Returns an error, vertex with ~id of 3 already exists

g.V(1).next();         // Returns vertex with ~id of 1, this is the vertex with name Lyndon. This is done with a direct lookup of the vertex record.
g.V(1, 2, 3).toList(); // Returns vertices with ~id of 1, 2, and 3, this is all 3 vertices that were created. This is done with a batch read of the vertex records.

g.addV("person").property("id", 4).property("name", "Joe").iterate();     // Creates vertex with label 'person', property 'id' with value 4, and property 'name' with value 'Joe'

g.V(4).next();             // This fails, there is no vertex with ~id of 4.
g.V().has("id", 4).next(); // Returns vertex with property 'id' with value 4, this is the vertex with name Joe. This requires running a secondary index query or scan.
```


